there was a time before april of 2020  when  port-au-pic nova scotia was known simply  as a quiet safe nova scotia community  where  not much happened and there was a time  when the rcmp was seen as a brave and  dependable force  an enduring symbol of strength famous  around the world  the mounties known for their red surge  uniforms and for always getting their  man  much of that was a romantic notion  poor depict now joins places like  moncton new brunswick  and mayor thorpe alberta places  synonymous with crisis and tragedy  including for the rcmp those mass  killings resulted in many injuries but  little change there is a need  for a lot of work moving forward to  improve and reform the rcmp so  how did we get here well the mounties  may be the only police force in the  world whose wikipedia entry states  they have an unusually large breadth of  duties  they enforce federal laws fight  organized crime  provide domestic and counter-terrorism  security as well as protective services  for vips  they also deliver police services at the  federal provincial and  municipal level they are the second  biggest police force in north america  but compared with other forces here in  canada they are underpaid  understaffed and overworked so  is there a way to fix it gary clement  has some ideas  he's one of the leading authorities on  policing in this country  he now runs a private investigative  agency focused on white-collar crime  after spending three decades with the  rcmp retiring at the rank of  superintendent  we talked to him from his home outside  ottawa  gary the whole country was horrified by  what happened in nova scotia not just  the murders  of course but the police response as a  former  cop yourself what was your gut reaction  well  at the outset i mean and i want to  preface this by saying it's really easy  to play  armchair quarterback after the fact but  all that being said  my my initial reaction is what you need  to have  after in a catastrophic event like this  uh is leadership and you need to have  people that get in front of the camera  and provide that sense of calm to the  community  and if i was to give points for the rcmp  on that initial  they failed miserably it is interesting  what you say about the media i'm sure  you've heard this from other reporters  is that  in terms of us dealing with the rcmp  it's basically uh  you know it's like hitting your head  against a brick wall often you just  don't get any information  and the kind of joke is that if you if  there's anything happening near the  border and you want to find out what's  happening you go ask the fbi because  they'll tell you  i made it a suggestion when i was  i think i at that time was an inspector  that we should look at hiring a  a journalist that was well known to  really lead us  because it was recognized years ago down  and the rcmp was doing a terrible job  this is just one of the symptoms of the  problems with the rcmp  if we were to look at what happens in  the united states  what you see is the leadership gets out  in front of these situations  they address it they give the public  some confidence  and there's all this secrecy wrapped  around it creates  uh confusion adds that there's a lot of  questions  raises the concerns of a major cover-up  when  9 10's out of 10 that's not the case but  the rcmp brings it on themselves  there is a chronic shortage of rcmp  officers in that region  and in other areas of canada too do you  think that potentially played a role in  the response  absolutely there's been a systemic  failure in the part of governments  to properly fund the rcmp and all of its  uh  requirements to for enforcement measures  is it time for the rcmp to get out of  municipal and provincial policing my  view is it's  it's long overdue and i say this just  because  um we and and i used reference  without going too far off topic donna  but  if we look at what's happened and what  the findings are in the cullen  commission  we're becoming a laughingstock when it  comes to traditional organized crime and  our ability to shut it down  when it comes to money laundering and  that really is the jurisdiction of the  rcmp but it's being pulled in so many  directions  that my view as the rcmp today is in a  crisis and  it's time that we accept that and the  government does something  to make sure that we keep the citizens  of canada safe  you know the circumstances in puerto  pick the horrible crimes  the gunmen committed were unlike  anything  we'd seen in canada have we learned from  that  now do you think would a response be  different  if something so horrific was to happen  again today i  without a doubt i mean i'm sure there's  a lot of  rcmp management that are beating  themselves up over this this isn't  something that you  just shun off and say you know nothing's  going to change i mean  the rcmp is having a hard look at it but  again i've got to go back donna that  we got to realize the rcmp cannot be all  things to all people and if we're going  to be more effective  we really should be honest with  ourselves recognize that  maybe it's time that we let provincial  and municipal  governments have deal with the policing  issue in their own backyard  and let the rcmp deal with the federal  very much like fbi or the drug  enforcement administration  united states so they they can be more  effective  and at the same time support our  provincial  and municipal partners because they look  to the rcmp to take on these higher  level investigations  and sadly they're failing miserably  right now
 

